---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Speaking — Tom Dimnet"
  description="Talks on what a good course refuses to teach, and why you can't train a trainer with a slide deck. French and English, based in Paris, happy to travel."
  current="speaking"
>
  <div class="region">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <h1 class="page">Speaking</h1>
    </div>

    <section class="mvt" style="padding-top:48px;">
      <p class="beat" style="margin-top:0;">
        A course is judged by what it refuses to teach. A system, by what it
        refuses to add.
      </p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          I've spent thirteen years in front of rooms — classrooms, bootcamps,
          engineering teams — and I'm now building a training program for
          engineers at a cloud company. I talk about the part of teaching nobody
          prepares you for: deciding what to leave out, and holding a room when
          your plan stops helping.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dry">
      <p class="dry-kicker">Talks</p>
      <div class="abstract">
        <h3>What a Good Course Refuses to Teach</h3>
        <p>
          Most failed trainings suffer from the same thing: too much content.
          Not because the trainer knew too much, but because nobody ever asked
          them to decide what mattered. I walk through real courses — Docker,
          design patterns — and show the decisions behind them: what I cut, what
          I kept, and why the order matters more than the list. For anyone who
          has to explain their work to someone else: mentors, tech leads, and
          yes, trainers.
        </p>
        <p class="tech">30–40 min · also works as a 15-min short</p>
      </div>
      <div class="abstract">
        <h3>You Can't Train a Trainer With a Slide Deck</h3>
        <p>
          Handing someone your slides doesn't make them a trainer — it makes
          them a narrator. Three levels of what "train the trainer" can mean:
          content, methodology, and the one almost nobody works on — posture,
          what you do when the room goes quiet and you can't tell whether
          they're thinking or lost. Drawn from building a training program for
          engineers, including the parts I haven't solved yet.
        </p>
        <p class="tech">30–40 min</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dry">
      <p class="dry-kicker">Previously</p>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>AWS Summit Paris</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          Two Datadog partner sessions on the main program — a log strategy that
          lets you sleep, and shipping features on Datadog Apps.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>DevOps D-Day</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          Conference talk. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dS9Hsti1OJo"
            >Watch ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>The Product Crew</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          Guest, on building and teaching. <a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SsCAZhUaTM8">Watch ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>training-dev.fr</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          Interview on training developers. <a
            href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gknHY5nlC6s">Watch ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>Le Joboscope — OpenClassrooms</h3>
        <p class="meta">
          Podcast episode. <a
            href="https://open.spotify.com/episode/11Mjn1lKEhz6FH1qaBbkpg?si=b4869a203c874fb7"
            >Listen ↗</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="item">
        <h3>Ironhack</h3>
        <p class="meta">Talks on how developers actually get hired.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dry practical">
      <p class="dry-kicker">Practical</p>
      <p>I speak in French and English. Based in Paris, happy to travel.</p>
      <p>Conferences, meetups, internal engineering sessions, podcasts.</p>
      <p class="meta" style="margin-top:20px;">
        Tell me the audience and I'll tell you honestly whether I'm the right
        person.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .abstract {
    padding: 40px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .abstract:first-of-type {
    border-top: 0;
  }
  .abstract h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(22px, 2.6vw, 27px);
    line-height: 1.18;
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 24rem;
  }
  .abstract p {
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .abstract .tech {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--meta);
    font-size: 14px;
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }
  .practical p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
</style>
